[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue with one fix queued. Its reports held two problems, and they ended differently. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October.

<Callout>
<code>parser</code> is fixed by <code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>. <code>hint</code> is won't fix, because the <code>booking-v3</code> form is retired. The parser fix did not fix <code>hint</code>. Retiring the form removes it.
</Callout>

## The two problems

<CauseTable ticket={4471} {filed} />

**`parser`, fixed:** The shared parser read every date month first, so the dates of day-first tenants came out flipped. This reached every tenant and every channel. [Ticket 1](https://github.com/HugoDulce/MegaDev-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1) is closed as completed.

**`hint`, won't fix:** The `booking-v3` form printed `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then read the date day first. People typed what the hint said, so their dates came out flipped too. Only form reports from day-first tenants on `booking-v3` had this cause. [Ticket 2](https://github.com/HugoDulce/MegaDev-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2) is closed as not planned.

## How to read the counts

- The table is computed when the page renders, by `causes.ts`. Do not retype its numbers.
- The cause comes from the tenant and the channel, not from `intakeTag`. Intake guesses from the reporter's browser, and the table shows how often it disagrees.
- The export repeats some reports, because its pages overlap. The table counts each id once.

## What is still unconfirmed

- **The fix's reach.** The fix names EU locales. `harbour.dental` writes dates in `en-GB`, which is day first but not an EU locale. Check that the fix covers that tenant.
- **The one-day reports.** The reports noted under the table do not look like a flip. A flip cannot turn 12 May into 11 May. The desk files them under `parser` by tenant, and this page keeps that rule, but nobody has confirmed their cause. If they keep coming after the fix, they need their own ticket.

## Why one ticket hid this

Both problems produced the same complaint, so their subject lines matched. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject alone, and the [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) repeats that rule. A decision is never edited, so the desk needs a new record that replaces it: merge on cause, not on subject.

